  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Monday) \nIssued at 1140 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nOne big weather switch flipped last week when we had the end of \n100 degree days and cooled down to normal. Another big switch will\nflip late this week as abundant atmospheric moisture surges into \nthe area. We are starting the week with precipitable water values \naround the 10th percentile, but by Saturday, we will be around \nthe 95th percentile. This will set the stage for a rainy weekend.\n\nSeveral systems are coming together to bring the suddenly\nunsettled pattern. The first will be a disturbance with a pocket \nof tropical moisture coming up from the Gulf. The next will be a \nnorthern stream shortwave and front. Finally, the biggest feature\nwill be a big southern stream low pressure system coming from the\nwest. This system will be pre-loaded with Pacific tropical \nmoisture. The interplay of these systems will make for a complex \nsetup determining exactly when and how much rain we get. \n\nAlthough there are model uncertainties about the evolution of this\nweekend's rain and how much we will receive, GEFS/ENS/GEPS\nensembles are pointing toward a soaking rainfall with more than 1\ninch likely. There is a 40 percent chance for more than 2 inches\nand a 20 percent chance for more than 3 inches. Rainfall is\nexpected to be showery with limited thunderstorm activity, but\nsome heavy downpours may occur, especially Saturday afternoon\nthrough Sunday morning. We should be able to handle a lot of \nrain, but we will be watching for localized flood potential if \nsome heavy bands set up. \n\nEventually the northern stream system will drop a front through\nthe area, sweeping out the heaviest rainfall. This may be Sunday \nor Monday. It looks like cooler air will follow the front, perhaps\nwith some post-frontal gloomy skies to start next week.",
